  "body": "Converter Rounding  Another deficiency in the converter interface is that it doesn't round off its result, leading sometimes to numbers with 20 or so digits. Modify the milesToKm() method below to fix this problem.   public class MetricConverter { public static double milesToKm(double miles) { \/\/ Change this code to use rounding return miles \/ 0.62; } public static void main(String[] args) { double miles = 100; System.out.println(miles + \" miles in kms = \" + MetricConverter.milesToKm(miles)); } }  To round a double you could use the Math.round() method introduced in Ch. 5.   To round a double you could use the Math.round() method. For example, suppose the number you want to round is d . Then the expression Math.round(100 * d)\/100.0 will round to two decimal places. Alternatively, you could use the java.text.NumberFormat class. Both of these approaches were covered in Chapter 5.   "
